**Output Note:** The true value is **532,638**. Notice how poorly the naive SRS estimate performs (predicting over 800,000) because it ignores county sizes. Both the SRS Ratio and SRS Regression methods perform much better by incorporating auxiliary population data, as does the HH estimator above, which uses the county sizes at the design stage.

**Summary.** The estimates of the total number of physicians in the US from all the methods above:


::: {#tbl-statepop-summary .cell tbl-cap='Estimates of the total number of physicians in the US (true value 532,638)'}
::: {.cell-output-display}


|Method                                              | Estimate|      SE| 95% CI lower| 95% CI upper| Estimate $-$ true|
|:---------------------------------------------------|--------:|-------:|------------:|------------:|-----------------:|
|Sample total, $N\bar{y}$ (SRS)                      |  933,411| 491,983|      -42,790|    1,909,612|           400,773|
|Ratio estimator (SRS)                               |  639,506|  87,885|      465,123|      813,889|           106,868|
|Regression estimator (SRS)                          |  585,871| 105,177|      377,149|      794,592|            53,233|
|Hansen--Hurwitz, $t_x\hat{B}_{\mathrm{HH}}$ (UPSWR) |  570,304|  41,401|      488,155|      652,453|            37,666|


:::
:::


The naive SRS total ignores the county sizes: it is far too large, with a huge SE. The ratio and regression
estimators use the county populations at the estimation stage and have much smaller SEs, and HH, which uses
them at the design stage, has the smallest SE and the smallest error here, even though its sample is of the same
size, 100 counties. @sec-ups-compare explains when using the size variable at the design stage pays off.

### The HH Estimator of the Total in Terms of $\psi_i$ {#sec-ups-hh-psi}

The selection probabilities need not come from a size variable: any $\psi_i>0$ with
$\sum_{i=1}^N\psi_i=1$ define a UPSWR design. Let $y_j^*$ and $\psi_j^*$ be the study value and the
selection probability of the unit selected on draw $j$. Dividing $y_j^*$ by its selection probability gives
an estimate of the total from that draw alone,
$$
u_j=\frac{y_j^*}{\psi_j^*},\qquad
E(u_j)=\sum_{i=1}^N\psi_i\,\frac{y_i}{\psi_i}=\sum_{i=1}^N y_i=t ,
$$
and the $u_j$ are iid, since the draws are independent. The HH estimator of the total is their average:
$$
\boxed{\hat{t}_{\mathrm{HH}}=\bar u=\frac1n\sum_{j=1}^n\frac{y_j^*}{\psi_j^*},\qquad
\widehat{\mathrm{SE}}(\hat{t}_{\mathrm{HH}})=\frac{s_u}{\sqrt n},\qquad
s_u^2=\frac{1}{n-1}\sum_{j=1}^n\left(\frac{y_j^*}{\psi_j^*}-\hat{t}_{\mathrm{HH}}\right)^2},
$$
with variance
$$
V(\hat{t}_{\mathrm{HH}})=\frac1n\sum_{i=1}^N\psi_i\left(\frac{y_i}{\psi_i}-t\right)^2 .
$$
The mean is estimated by $\hat{t}_{\mathrm{HH}}/N$. A unit with a small $\psi_i$ is rarely drawn, but when it is, its value
is multiplied by the large factor $1/\psi_i$; this is what makes $u_j$ unbiased. This form is what the
function `upswr_total` computes.

**Equivalence with $\hat{B}_{\mathrm{HH}}$.** The formula is the estimator of @sec-ups-hh-theory with the
selection probabilities themselves as the size variable. Take the **normalized size**
$$
\tilde{x}_i=\psi_i,\qquad\text{so that}\qquad t_{\tilde{x}}=\sum_{i=1}^N\tilde{x}_i=1
\quad\text{and}\quad \frac{\tilde{x}_i}{t_{\tilde{x}}}=\psi_i .
$$
The ratios and the population ratio for this size variable are
$$
\tilde{b}_i=\frac{y_i}{\tilde{x}_i}=\frac{y_i}{\psi_i},\qquad
\tilde{B}=\frac{t}{t_{\tilde{x}}}=t ,
$$
so each observation $\tilde{b}_j^*=y_j^*/\psi_j^*=u_j$, and
$$
\hat{\tilde{B}}_{\mathrm{HH}}=\frac1n\sum_{j=1}^n\tilde{b}_j^*=\hat{t}_{\mathrm{HH}},\qquad
\hat{t}_{\mathrm{HH}}=t_{\tilde{x}}\,\hat{\tilde{B}}_{\mathrm{HH}}=\hat{\tilde{B}}_{\mathrm{HH}},\qquad
s_{\tilde{b}^*}=s_u .
$$
With a normalized size, the "ratio" $\tilde{B}$ *is* the total, and the estimator, its SE and its variance are
those of @sec-ups-hh-theory. Conversely, when $\psi_i=x_i/t_x$, every draw gives
$y_j^*/\psi_j^*=t_x\,y_j^*/x_j^*=t_x\,b_j^*$, so $\hat{t}_{\mathrm{HH}}=t_x\,\bar{b}^*$ and
$s_u=t_x\,s_{b^*}$, the formulas for the total in @sec-ups-hh-theory. Rescaling a size variable to
total 1 therefore does not change the estimator of the total, since $\psi_i$ depends on $x_i$ only through
$x_i/t_x$.

#### Example: Revenue from AI Token Usage, Using $\psi_i$ {#sec-ups-hh-psi-example}

We repeat the AI token example of @sec-ups-toy-sample with the formula in terms of $\psi_i$. The same sample
$\mathcal{R}=\{F,E,F,D,C\}$ is used, but each draw now gives an estimate $u_j=y_j^*/\psi_j^*$ of the **total**
revenue, and the HH estimate is their average.


::: {.cell}
::: {.cell-output-display}


| Draw $j$|Customer | $y_j^*$| $\psi_j^*$| $u_j=y_j^*/\psi_j^*$| $u_j-\hat{t}_{\mathrm{HH}}$| $(u_j-\hat{t}_{\mathrm{HH}})^2$|
|--------:|:--------|-------:|----------:|--------------------:|---------------------------:|-------------------------------:|
|        1|F        |     190|       0.40|                475.0|                       -14.2|                           200.7|
|        2|E        |     155|       0.30|                516.7|                        27.5|                           756.3|
|        3|F        |     190|       0.40|                475.0|                       -14.2|                           200.7|
|        4|D        |      70|       0.15|                466.7|                       -22.5|                           506.2|
|        5|C        |      41|       0.08|                512.5|                        23.3|                           544.4|
|         |**Sum**  |        |           |              2,445.8|                         0.0|                         2,208.3|


:::
:::


**Step 1: HH estimate of the total.**
$$
\hat{t}_{\mathrm{HH}}=\bar{u}=\frac1n\sum_{j=1}^n\frac{y_j^*}{\psi_j^*}=\frac{2445.8}{5}=489.2,
$$
the same as $t_x\hat{B}_{\mathrm{HH}}=489.2$ in @sec-ups-toy-sample.

**Step 2: its standard error.** From the last column,
$$
s_u^2=\frac{1}{n-1}\sum_{j=1}^5\left(u_j-\hat{t}_{\mathrm{HH}}\right)^2=\frac{2208.3}{4}
=552.1,\qquad
\widehat{\mathrm{SE}}(\hat{t}_{\mathrm{HH}})=\frac{s_u}{\sqrt{n}}=10.5,
$$
again equal to $t_x\,s_{b^*}/\sqrt{n}=10.5$ (here $u_j=t_x\,b_j^*$).

**Step 3: the mean and $B$.** Divide by $N$ and by $t_x$:
$\hat{\bar{y}}_{\mathrm{HH}}=\hat{t}_{\mathrm{HH}}/N=81.53$ and
$\hat{B}_{\mathrm{HH}}=\hat{t}_{\mathrm{HH}}/t_x=4.8917$.

The same estimates from `upswr_est`, with the normalized size $\tilde{x}_i=\psi_i$ of @sec-ups-hh-psi, whose
total is $t_{\tilde{x}}=1$: the ratios are the $u_j$, and their mean $\hat{\tilde{B}}_{\mathrm{HH}}$ is
$\hat{t}_{\mathrm{HH}}$ itself. So `xbar_pop` and `tx_pop` are left out, and the call returns the estimate of the
total; the estimates of $\bar{y}_U$ and $B$ follow by dividing the estimate and its SE by $N=6$ and by
$t_x=100$:


::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
token$psi <- token$x / 100   # psi_i = x_i / t_x
t_hat <- upswr_est(token$y, token$psi, show.details = FALSE)$estimate   # estimate of t
t_hat |> format_est_gt("hh_total")
```

